WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Node.js applications sometimes call external processes for image processing,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.810
document conversion, system utilities, build steps, or operational scripts.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.905
Command injection risk appears when user-controlled input influences the command,

00:00:12.905 --> 00:00:17.240
arguments, options, environment, file paths, or working directory in an unsafe way.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.245
The danger increases when code builds one shell string and asks the operating system to

00:00:21.245 --> 00:00:25.010
interpret it. A safer design starts by asking whether a shell is needed at all.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:28.280
Many tasks can be performed through libraries, platform APIs,

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.725
or process execution modes that pass a fixed executable and controlled arguments

00:00:32.725 --> 00:00:36.560
separately. Avoiding shell interpretation removes a large class of ambiguity.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.640
When external execution is necessary,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.560
the set of allowed operations should be narrow and explicit. Argument handling matters.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.630
User choices should map to allowlisted operations, file types,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.160
or options rather than becoming free-form command text.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.440
File paths should be resolved carefully within intended directories.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.240
Environment variables should be controlled.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.400
The process should run with the least privilege practical for the task,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.765
and the working directory should not be left to chance.

00:00:58.765 --> 00:01:02.115
Logging should support investigation without leaking sensitive data.

00:01:02.115 --> 00:01:06.080
It is useful to record which approved operation was requested, who requested it,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.350
and whether it succeeded.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.270
It is not useful to dump secrets, full command strings containing private values,

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.565
or untrusted input into logs without thought.

00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:15.870
Observability and privacy need to be balanced.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.555
The defensive principle is to avoid string-built commands,

00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:21.829
separate commands from arguments, constrain what users can request,

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:26.400
prefer safer libraries when practical, and treat process execution as a sensitive sink.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.690
The more powerful the command environment, the stricter the design should be.
